 - Bash: cat .git-blame-ignore-revs; git ls-files | grep -v '^vendor/' | wc -l; git ls-files | grep -c '^vendor/'; git shortlog -sne HEAD | head -15
 - Bash: git ls-files | grep -v '^vendor/' ; echo ---; for f in $(git ls-files | grep -v '^vendor/' | grep -E '\.go$'); do git blame -w --ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs --line-porcelain HEAD -- "$f" | grep '^author-mail '; done | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | awk '{t+=$1; c[NR]=$1; n[NR]=$3} END{for(i=1;i<=NR;i++) printf "%5d %5.1f%% %s\n", c[i], 100*c[i]/t, n[i]; print t, "total"}' | head -15
FINAL ANSWER:
Ownership is concentrated in one person, but not in a single-person way. I ran `git blame` on the live lines of all 6 `.go` files, which total 1,655 lines. I used `-w`, and I skipped the `interface{}` to `any` commit through `.git-blame-ignore-revs`.

| Rank | Author (blame email) | Lines | Share |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Fatih Arslan (`<email redacted>`) | 823 | 49.7% |
| 2 | `<email redacted>` | 144 | 8.7% |
| 3 | `<email redacted>` | 103 | 6.2% |
| 4 | `<email redacted>` | 98 | 5.9% |
| 5 | `<email redacted>` | 97 | 5.9% |

- **Top owner:** Fatih Arslan owns about half the live code. By commit count he is far ahead: 201 commits, against 38 for the next entry, which is dependabot.
- **Who comes next:** The second-largest owner, `rhysd`, holds under 9%, so no one else is close to him.
- **Spread:** About 15 other people own between 1% and 6% each. The code is shared across many contributors, and one person holds half of it.

- **Authors with several emails:** I did not merge these. `<email redacted>` (3.3%) and `<email redacted>` (2.4%) may each be the same person under another address. The ranking does not change if they are merged.
- **Generated file:** `attribute_string.go` is generated by a tool, so some blame lines come from the commit that added it, not from hand-written code.
- **Excluded files:** I left out `vendor/`, which has 2 files, and non-Go files such as the README and workflow files.

The repo is small, about 1,650 lines of Go. If the maintainer stopped, a fork would be cheap to take over. The risk is less about who owns the code and more about who is still merging fixes. I can check recent commit dates and who has merge rights if you want that.

